Make the LZO-compressed kernel payload reproducible.

lzop stores a modification time in its header. Reading the payload from a
pipe leaves lzop no source file to take that time from, so it stores the
current clock and the header checksum that covers it. Two builds of an
identical arch/arm/boot/Image therefore produce different piggy.lzo files,
and the difference propagates into zImage and the final uImage.

Compress a scratch copy of the input instead, with its mode and modification
time pinned first. lzop then records those fixed values.

The copy matters. Pinning the times on the input itself would leave
arch/arm/boot/Image permanently older than the vmlinux it is built from, so
if_changed would rebuild the whole objcopy, compress, link, and image tail on
every later make even with no source change.

The kernel's own LZO decompressor is unaffected. parse_header() in
lib/decompress_unlzo.c skips the mode, both mtime words, the file name, and
the header checksum, so recording a name and a zero timestamp changes
nothing it reads.

--- a/scripts/Makefile.lib
+++ b/scripts/Makefile.lib
@@ -303,9 +303,11 @@
 	(rm -f $@ ; false)
 
 quiet_cmd_lzo = LZO     $@
-cmd_lzo = (cat $(filter-out FORCE,$^) | \
-	lzop -9 && $(call size_append, $(filter-out FORCE,$^))) > $@ || \
-	(rm -f $@ ; false)
+cmd_lzo = (cp $(filter-out FORCE,$^) $@.in && chmod 0644 $@.in && \
+	touch -d @0 $@.in && lzop -9 -c $@.in && \
+	$(call size_append, $(filter-out FORCE,$^))) > $@ && \
+	rm -f $@.in || \
+	(rm -f $@ $@.in ; false)
 
 # U-Boot mkimage
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
